{"attack": true, "case": "The target's defense depends entirely on eliminative logic: preference is rational because the preferred theory is 'the only one left standing.' But this premise is empirically and conceptually false for the domain it purports to cover. In the actual ecosystem of science, and even within Popper's own framework, multiple unfalsified theories typically coexist at any given time. Popper's preference is not for the *sole* survivor but for the *most* corroborated among several survivors — a comparative ranking that requires criteria beyond mere survival. The target's evolutionary analogy collapses at this point: in natural selection, extinction removes competitors decisively, but in science, falsified theories can be revived, modified, or coexist with rivals. The target's own counterconditions betray this weakness by only considering the extremes (zero survivors or a survivor implying future fitness) while omitting the typical and decisive case: when multiple theories survive, the 'description of survival' yields no preference at all, since survival is binary and cannot rank among the living. The account thus provides no basis for the very preference it claims to defend whenever the field of competitors is not reduced to one — which is the normal condition of science.", "counterexample": ["Consider two theories, A and B, both of which have survived all available severe tests. Under the target's framework, both are 'survivors' — both are 'left standing.' The target's mechanism says preference is rational because the preferred theory is 'the only one left standing.' But neither is the only one standing. The account therefore yields no basis for preferring A over B, yet Popper explicitly holds that we should prefer the more severely tested one. The survival-description cannot produce this ranking, so the defense fails for the standard multi-survivor case."], "premise": "The target's defense reduces preference to the fact that a theory is the sole survivor ('the only one left standing'), but Popper's actual preference is for the *most* corroborated theory among *multiple* surviving rivals, which the binary survival metric cannot adjudicate.", "premise_evidence": [{"block": "4abb9c7b7f85c691e311b6567a16f6219f82b4b6700746685044bea1adbfd264", "quote": "Preferring the survivor is rational because it is the only one left standing, not because we expect it to survive tomorrow."}], "successor_question": "When multiple theories have survived all available severe tests, what does the survival-description framework offer as a basis for preferring one over the others, and how is that basis anything other than an inductive or comparative judgment that transcends mere 'description of survival'?"}